    "stop_id": "jozefa_street",
    "title": "Józefa Street",
    "description": "Józefa Street is at the heart of Kazimierz’s old Jewish quarter, running through an area that was once the vibrant center of Jewish life in Kraków. Established as part of the Jewish town (Oppidum Judaeorum) in the late 15th century, this street and its surroundings became home to synagogues, mikvehs, kosher butcheries, schools, and the homes of rabbis and wealthy merchants. The Jewish quarter was separated from Christian Kazimierz by a stone wall, and Józefa Street marked the border of this unique, autonomous community.\n\nToday, Józefa Street is a lively mix of history and bohemian spirit. As you stroll along, you’ll find some of the oldest synagogues in the district, including the High Synagogue (Wysoka), whose prayer hall is on the first floor, and quirky shops, bookshops, and galleries that reflect the creative energy of modern Kazimierz. The street is also famous for its street art and murals, especially around number 17, and is home to small art studios like Lookarna Illustrations at number 11, where local artists display and sell their works.\n\nJózefa Street is not only a showcase for Jewish heritage but also a hub for Kraków’s contemporary creative scene. Murals and art installations line the walls, and the area buzzes with cafes, bars, and boutiques. The old Jewish town proper begins where Józefa meets Jakuba Street, near the High Synagogue, marking the historic gateway into the Oppidum Judaeorum. This blend of tradition and creativity makes Józefa Street a must-visit for anyone wanting to experience the layered history and dynamic present of Kazimierz.\n\nWhether you’re exploring its historic synagogues, browsing art studios, or simply soaking up the vibrant atmosphere, Józefa Street offers a unique window into the soul of Kraków’s most fascinating district.\n\nOur next stop: High Synagogue. Completed in 1563 in the late Renaissance style, the High Synagogue is one of Kazimierz’s most distinctive landmarks, known for its unique upper-floor prayer hall and rich Jewish heritage.",
